A. Design Optimization Methods: A Brief Overview
Optimization processes attempt to find the best possible design solution amongst all available solutions.8 Design optimization chooses a set of design features (or a system architecture) from a range of feasible options (called the ‘design space’) that minimizes or maximizes some objective function, subject to a set of defined constraints. Typically, an optimal architecture must take into account many facets of system performance that are important to its users, such as quality, safety, reliability, and maintainability. Multiple objective optimization (MOO) (a.k.a. vector or Pareto optimization) techniques are used for design problems with more than one objective function. For example, one might want to minimize cost, while also maximizing product reliability. If objectives conflict with one another (as they often do), there can be no design solution that optimizes all of them at the same time. The “Pareto optimum solution set” includes the combination of architectures for which any further improvement in one objective will result in degradation of another objective. The Pareto set lies along the boundary of the feasible design space, known as the Pareto front, illustrated in Figure 1. The “optimal” design then becomes a choice along the Pareto front, based on the relative importance of the objectives. Design optimization requires definition of many parameters and processes: a feasible design space or set of design parameters; objective functions (i.e. evaluation criteria) for comparing design solutions; methods for assessing the objectives for a specific design; methods for searching through the design space; and methods for choosing an optimal solution or set of solutions. Historically, spacecraft design has been heavily dependent upon the use of heritage systems. Existing flightproven designs are modified to fit new requirements, achieving feasibility but not optimality.9 The number of potential design variables and objective functions for a spacecraft configuration (and all of its subsystems) are prohibitive for optimizing across the entire design space. In addition, the response surfaces for spacecraft performance are often very complex and require significant user involvement to develop.10 For these reasons, spacecraft architects have sought to reduce the size of the design space and therefore computation resources using metaheuristic methods, such as genetic algorithms or simulated annealing.9,10,11,12 ECLSS is a complex spacecraft system, with highly coupled subsystems and components, which might benefit from the application of multi-disciplinary and metaheuristic methods during architecture development. Several studies have demonstrated the use of optimization algorithms for ECLSS technology trades, configuration comparisons, and system sizing.13,14,15,16,17,18,19
B. Feasible ECLSS Architecture Identification
A feasible design solution is one that satisfies hard constraints that are essential, as opposed to soft constraints that users or designers would like to satisfy but are not essential.8 Early identification of such hard constraints limits the design space for subsequent optimization. Technical performance, as well as cost and schedule constraints typically drive ECLSS feasibility studies during early mission planning.20 At this stage in design, steady state, back of the envelope estimates of system performance and cost are sufficient to identify candidate architectures and reduce the design space before more detailed iteration.21 Technologies chosen for exploration missions might borrow directly or evolve from those used aboard the International Space Station (ISS), or develop from recent technical advances to address changing needs and demands.22 Even in the face of cost, schedule, and mass constraints, the use of heritage systems may not meet the technical requirements for longer duration missions. To aid in feasible architecture
