C. Performance Metrics for ECLSS Comparison
After identifying the feasible design space designers must then choose the “best” solution. When more than one design option meeting all requirements is available, soft constraints, also known as objectives, guide the architectural comparisons. Soft constraints contribute to a higher quality system and typically become objective functions for design optimization (such as maximizing reliability or minimizing cost). These objectives, often referred to as ‘-ilities’, reflect the ability of the system to meet the users’ needs and expectations. Measures of effectiveness (MOE) are the specific metrics used to measure how well the system achieves its objectives (a.k.a. Measures of Performance, Performance Metrics, Performance Indicators, or Figures of Merit). Typical objectives address the systems effectiveness and its cost, to varying degrees. However, cost and effectiveness criteria usually conflict requiring multiobjective optimization techniques. Ideally, all vested stakeholders (designers, users, investors, etc.) are involved in choosing and prioritizing these criteria, herein referred to as performance metrics. Designers can define an apriori utility function based on the relative importance of each metric or present decision makers with a set of Pareto optimal solutions. For ECLSS evaluation, analysts must first define successful operation as well as cost. ECLSS architecture trades typically compare mass, reliability, and closure. For long missions stored consumables, levels of closure, and spare parts to improve reliability are major drivers of system mass.20,23 The most common ECLSS performance metrics are described in detail below. 1. Equivalent System Mass (ESM)
ESM is NASA’s standard measure for evaluating ECLSS system cost, and it is by far the mostly widely used metric for ECLSS trade studies in technical literature. Originally developed by Ref. 24, ESM includes the mass of a system and associated infrastructure given a specific mission location, duration, and crew size.25 ESM approximates system launch cost as a sum of actual mass (initial infrastructure plus consumables) and equivalent masses of volume, power, cooling, and crew time. Ref.’s 25-26 define the metric in detail and provide guidance for implementation. If the technologies under comparison have the same function at the same quality, reliability, and safety, the best option becomes that with the lowest ESM.25 Researchers must apply their own expertise to adjust calculations for equal quality, reliability, and safety by adding mass for spare parts, consumables, tools, design modifications, etc. ESM has the advantage of allowing comparison of multiple systems with one utility function that incorporates all primary factors affecting system mass. If used correctly, by incorporated mass to achieve equal reliability, it also accounts for system performance. Other advantages of ESM are its applicability to systems, sub-systems, and components; understandability and ease of computation; and objectivity and precision allowing meaningful technology comparison. ESM has some disadvantages (listed below), however, that make it a better criterion for early architecture (Phase A) development than for detailed (Phase B/C) system design optimization. ESM provides a rough order of magnitude, best used in early mission planning. 1) Large Design Space:7 Because the ESM of an integrated system is not necessarily equal to the summed ESM of its parts (in the case of multifunctional components), all possible combinations of technologies must be traded against one another, resulting in a large design space for optimization. 2) System Scaling and Stability:27 Analysts must take care in system scaling, by sizing equipment to achieve steady state flow rates through the system and mass balance. The validity of ESM is therefore dependent upon the quality of the sizing analysis. 3) Crew Time Bias: Though the addition of crew time mass is an excellent way to incorporate the maintenance burden of a system into one utility function, the calculation of the crew time cost factor may create an unfair bias. A 50% reduction in available crew time might not necessarily require twice the system mass. 4) Location Dependence: Propulsion mass and hence launch cost changes with mission location. Analysts must only compare systems destined for the same location with the same propulsion type.21 5) Other Life Cycle Costs: Analysts should not use ESM as a standalone measure of system cost. ESM is proportional to launch cost, but it does not include costs for design, development, testing, and evaluation (DDT&E) or operations.21


