2. Life Cycle Cost (LCC)
Because costs for development, launch, and operations vary depending on the mission destination and duration, LCC is a more inclusive and accurate cost estimate than equivalent mass.21 According to the Systems Engineering Body of Knowledge (SEBoK), life cycle cost is “the total cost of implementation and ownership of a system over its useful life. It includes the cost of development, acquisition, operation, maintenance, support, and, where applicable, disposal.”6 LCC is a standard systems analysis metric used by NASA in mission design and throughout the aerospace industry.21 For space exploration missions, LCC will include DDT&E, launch and emplacement, and operations. Ideally, system disposal or retirement should also be included in LCC. Ref. 21and Ref. 28 provide an overview of methods for estimating the different components of LCC. 3. Closure
Closure reflects the ability of an ECLSS to continue functioning without resupply, defining its autonomy.14 The Index of System Closure (C) characterizes the portion of expended substance that is recycled (not stored) per unit time: C = 1- m/M, where m is the mass of stores consumed, and M is the total mass consumed.29 Designers might treat closure as a goal (rather than a requirement), as complete closure (C equal to one) is not practical to achieve.14 4. Stability
For stability, a steady state in mass balance must exist for each element in the system, in terms of O2, CO2, H2O, etc. Non-zero mass into the ECLSS must equal mass out or else deadlocks will result, eventually leading to system failure. Ref. 14 cautions that the index of closure can only be meaningful if there exists a settled stationary state and the total mass of the system is constant. Systems of differential equations describing mass flow dynamics between elements allow stability analysis. ECLSS analysts often consider stability in the context of biological life support systems, but it is also applicable and just as important for physico-chemical (P/C) systems. 5. Reliability
Reliable functioning ensures the safety of human life under space mission conditions.30 Mass based optimization assumes that a higher degree of recycling (or closure) is desirable for long duration missions. However, due to the inability to get replacement parts or other resupply from Earth if failure occurs, reliability may become more important than mass. Recycling (as opposed to storage) may not be the most reliable means of providing for the crew’s life support.31 The need for extreme ECLSS reliability for long duration missions is hardly a new revelation. In 1968, Ref. 32 stated,
“Reliability and maintainability problems confronting environmental control/life support systems for long duration space flight present the designer with one of the most challenging tasks in the wide array of still unproved approaches to the design of manned space systems.”
The Systems Engineering Body of Knowledge (SEBoK) defines reliability as “the probability of a system or system element performing its intended function under stated conditions without failure for a given period of time.”6 A failure is the inability to perform as intended. There are several metrics for reliability, including mean time to failure (MTTF) for non-repairable systems, mean time between failures (MTBF) for repairable systems, failure rate, or probability of failure. A reliability function R(t), also known as the survival function, is the probability that a unit (or system) survives beyond time t: R(t) = 1- F(t), where F(t) is the probability that a unit will fail by time t.33 F(t) is the cumulative distribution function of failure probability density function f(t), defined for t=0 to infinity. Failure rate, h(t), is the instantaneous rate of failure for survivors at time t during the next instance of time: h(t) = f(t)/R(t) = f(t)/(1F(t)). The integral of h(t) from 0 to time t is known as the cumulative hazard function.33 Ref. 31 defines ECLSS reliability as the probability of loss of crew (PrLOC) which is equal to the failure rate per day for life-threatening failures times the mission duration in days. The author suggests that PrLOC due to life support failure must be less than 1/1000 for all mission durations, such that the allowable failure rate per day decreases with mission duration. For a 3-year mission, this would require a daily failure rate on the order of 1E-6 for ECLSS subsystems or components, termed ultra-reliablity.34 Reliability Estimation: It is difficult to observe failures in testing, especially for highly reliable systems; hence, reliability engineers have developed modeling techniques to estimate failure rates from test data. Accelerated testing methods test components at high stress levels for failures to occur. Time to failure under normal use can then be predicted as a function of operating stress applied in testing. System reliability is determined as a function of the individual failure rates of the integrated components and their configuration. System models include combinations of series, parallel, standby, or R-out-of-N components. In a series model, one component failure leads to system failure. In a parallel model configuration, all components must fail before the system fails. A standby configuration contains an inactive redundant component that steps in if the active component fails. An R-out-of-N system requires R of the


