than replacement of an entire module) offer improved potential for extended reliability and that the ability of the crew to manage ECLSS is essential to successful system performance. ECLSS reliability analysis tools include Reliability Environment for Life-Support System Simulation and Analysis (RELISSA) developed by the Institute of Space Systems at the University of Stuttgart39 and the Exploration Maintainability Analysis Tool (EMAT) developed by NASA.40 6. Integral Reliability Criterion
Integral reliability30 is the summed probability of catastrophic ECLSS failure (PLSS), human fatal mistakes due to life support quality (PQL), and failure of supporting equipment such as power (PGMS), in general form:
PLSS(mass) + PQL(T) + PGMS(mass). (1)
PLSS typically decreases with increased life support system mass (mass). PQL is dependent upon quality of life and time (T) of stay in the habitat. PGMS(mass) increases with system mass. Integral reliability is an objective quantitative criterion that requires dynamic modeling and simulation to account for the effects of stochastic external disturbances, emergency scenarios, and perturbations in component performance over time.7 The mass term includes equipment and spare parts, consumables, or other components to improve reliability. The calculation of PQL is much more difficult to estimate, but assumptions could be made about the time dependent cognitive and physical impairment in a degraded environment and the resulting probability of fatal mistake. The mass dependent probability of failure for general mission support systems (PGMS) depends upon the mission scenario.
III. Cost of Uncertainty in ECLSS Design
As crews travel to distant unexplored environments, mission designers will need heightened confidence in life support functionality under increasing levels of uncertainty and risk. The top technical challenges for human spaceflight beyond low Earth orbit (LEO) are to create safety infrastructure, autonomous operational strategies and systems, highly reliable and maintainable systems, and robust systems to deal with the unexpected.22,41 This paper discusses the notion of robust in the context of uncertainty and proposes robustness as a necessary system characteristic that supersedes and includes the other challenges. In order to develop robust systems, characterization of the unexpected is a necessary first step.
A. Sources of Uncertainty Affecting ECLSS Performance
To meet the demands of long duration life support, ECLSS technology will undoubtedly become more complex and have longer operational life spans. As system complexity and life spans increase, the likelihood of encountering variability (internally or externally) also increases.42 Increased variability means increased uncertainty in component reliability, environmental operating conditions and hazards, crew metabolic loads, internal process parameters governing outputs, component interactions, and even mission characteristics. This uncertainty rises with mission duration and distance from earth.


