“Uncertainties are things that are not known, or known only imprecisely.”43
According to the SEBoK, uncertainty is the result of not having accurate or sufficient knowledge of a situation.6 Ref. 43 identifies two classes of uncertainty: lack of knowledge or lack of system definition. Many random events are probable (frequent and foreseen, statistically characterized events with relatively low impact). Other random events are possible (foreseeable events that occur less frequently but have higher impact). While some events are only plausible (very infrequent, very high impact rare or “black swan” events that may be unforeseeable by subject matter experts and are difficult to statistically characterize). Space is characterized by many uncertainties, natural and human induced, which might affect system functional performance, making theoretical reliability analysis difficult.44 Historical data and empirical evidence exists for the characterization of some, but not all sources of uncertainty. That does not mean however, that designs cannot accommodate the unknown. Designers can bound realistic possible ranges of environmental conditions, or types of rare events. Once this uncertainty is characterized, or at least bounded, a designer must then attempt to predict impact on ECLSS performance and on crew-wellbeing. How much performance degradation can be tolerated and for how long? What are tolerable conditions that the crew could be forced to operate in during an emergency? How does ECLSS performance under unexpected conditions affect the choice and optimization of system architecture? 1. Component Performance Definition
Component Failure Rates: Since component failure rates are empirical, treating failure rates as uncertain parameters may avoid unnecessarily high redundancy that results in heavier systems.11 Component failure rates are variable due to differences between individual units that result from imperfect manufacturing, differences in storage conditions and environmental exposures, material imperfections, etc. Failure rates are also time dependent and reliability uncertainty grows over time, due to the expense of collecting failure rate data over long durations. Component or Sub-system Process Rates: The low technology readiness or flight heritage of newer regenerable technologies results in higher uncertainty in performance parameters such as recovery rates or process efficiencies, especially over time of use. This is especially true for biological components. Uncertainty in forecasting system states, even if the mathematical description of state dynamics is well defined, occurs due to variability in the responses of individual organisms.14 Variation of process rates between individuals applies to the human crewmembers as well (e.g. metabolic rates, consumable utilization, and waste generation). Higher cycling rates are typical within a space habitat, amplifying and propagating the effects of these uncertainties throughout the system. Resource Requirements for Mass Estimates: ESM estimates for less mature technology are less accurate when performance and resource requirements are uncertain, and therefore must be adjusted based on technology maturity.45 Uncertain Effects of Space Environment: Experience with regenerative life support technology on the ISS has shown reduced gravity or microgravity to have unanticipated adverse effects on component performance. The radiation environment in LEO and beyond is difficult to replicate on Earth as well. Ground testing may not reveal unanticipated effects of space radiation on components. Only long duration flight-testing can verify component performance in the intended operating environment. 2. System Definition - Plant Model
Integrated System Performance: Fundamental relationships between system performance, component states, and environmental conditions are ill defined, especially for systems that include biological components or immature technology. System performance predictions require dynamic models of non-linear component interactions that drive emergent system level behavior. Benchtop component level experiments provide empirical model coefficients that are less certain than those derived from full-scale experiments. However, experimental data for integrated systems is expensive to collect. Dynamic Process Rates: Some mass and energy exchange processes have stochastic behavior, such as human metabolism, plant growth, heat exchange, gas diffusion, etc. Therefore, mass and energy flow rates throughout the system will exhibit transient behavior that may or may not be consistent or predictable. Transients in mass and energy exchange rates may result due to ill-defined complex component interactions. 3. Metric Uncertainty
Metrics are approximations or indicators of an objective. They also contain inaccuracies in their formulation and in the level of diligence applied to their calculation. For example, ESM inaccuracies result from location and vehicle dependent power, volume, or heat rejection conversion coefficients. 4. Operating Environment and Usage
Internal Operating Environment Instability: ECLSS performance controls the internal spacecraft environment, which in turn affects ECLSS performance. Uncertainty in system, sub-system, and component performance for ECLSS presents uncertainty in the internal operating environment of the spacecraft. Transients in gas composition, pressure, temperature, humidity, contamination, etc. may occur that could result in unexpected system loads.


