Actual In-flight Utilization and Procedures: Ref. 32 observed that differences in in-flight utilization and maintenance procedures result in large variability for MTBF in airline systems (more so than variability in quality between manufacturers). System usage in flight by the crew is a source of uncertainty that influences reliability of system performance. In this way, the crew is a system element upon which ECLSS performance depends. Boundary Conditions: Variable mass and energy exchange rates between the spacecraft and the environment include atmospheric leakage, energy inputs, and energy loss. External Operating Environment Uncertainty: Estimates of the expected radiation environment, micrometeorite exposure, temperature, and even gravity come from theoretical and empirical models based upon remote observations. Predictions of the anticipated space environment are likely quite good for the purposes of system design. However, as exploration missions take spacecraft into less traversed environments, the likelihood of unexpected environmental variability increases. Because the space environment is so extreme, the consequences of this uncertainty may be equally extreme, even if the uncertainty is small. The purpose of exploration is to gain knowledge. Thus, uncertainty in what we will find during the journey and when we get there is inherent to that exploration. Rare Events: Rare events are those with potentially widespread, severe impact that occur with low frequency, such as earthquakes, asteroid impacts, solar flares, etc. In aerospace engineering, rare events also apply to less catastrophic events that may happen at low probabilities of less than 1E-6.46 The likelihood of rare events (e.g. a large dust storm on Mars or a large meteor impact on an asteroid) increases as we move further into unexplored, little studied environments, simply because probability estimates have limited accuracy. 5. Mission Characteristics
The mission itself may be a source of uncertainty for exploration life support system design. More far reaching mission objectives may have inherent uncertainty in ECLSS design drivers such as mission duration, resupply intervals, and crew size. Simply the passage of time during long duration exploration increases the likelihood of mission changes in response to unanticipated events, such as funding shortages, launch vehicle problems, changes in science objectives based on new knowledge, etc. Even during early mission planning, system designs must be flexible to mission uncertainty. As the specific goal of exploration missions continues to shift (e.g. the moon, asteroids, or Mars), it becomes necessary to maintain a flexible path philosophy by designing architectures that are capability driven and encompass all mission possibilities.47
B. Cost of Uncertainty
Failures generate costs (during development, production, and operation), and failure costs increase the later they occur in the product life cycle. The cost of failure can be direct, such as the cost of production rework and scrap, and indirect, such as the cost of schedule delay or loss of sales.48 Failures can even lead to product recalls and litigation in the case of injury or death.48 Strategies to reduce failures and hence their cost have a price, called the cost of quality. Quality engineers attempt to find the minimum cost of quality, which may or may not occur before reaching 100% reliability. Modern experts tend to think that by eliminating the root causes of failure, the savings will always be greater than the cost of prevention.48 For space life support, increased uncertainty may result in unanticipated system behavior, increasing the likelihood of component faults, system failure, or even design obsolescence. The impact of safety critical system failures and the cost of their prevention also rise with mission distance and duration, due to launch mass and volume constraints, time and cost of resupply, and reduced ability to abort to Earth. Without multiple vehicles or abort capability, a system failure could result in a loss of crew and an entire campaign, rather than just a loss of vehicle or mission. Ref. 49 cautions that future life support system complexity (large mix of technologies with large variety and numbers of components) will lead to lower reliability and higher maintenance requirements than other systems.36,49 A number of studies for crewed space missions indicated that ECLSS generally requires more scheduled and unscheduled maintenance than any other system, and that this has been the case since the first crewed space missions.36,50 Traditional methods for improving reliability (such as incorporating redundancy or carrying spares) to achieve acceptable risk may also be too costly or ineffective to ensure system operability over long durations and uncertain conditions.51 The reduced ability to resupply or abort heightens the need for systems that can be repaired and maintained on-board. Spares will have to be flown with the spacecraft or pre-positioned, requiring a large mass and volume for storage and transport. In addition, a greater number of spares is required to accommodate increased uncertainty in component reliability.51 Other approaches to reduce the cost of uncertainty will be required, such as component commonality, functional redundancy, reduced complexity, and in-situ manufacturing.51,52,53


