IV. ECLSS Robustness: Performance under Perturbation
A. Robustness, Reliability, Resilience, and Survivability: What is the Difference? 1. The Change Related ‘-ilities’: Establishing a Semantic Frame of Reference
There is a plethora of design objectives (or ‘-ilities’) for engineered systems related to performance under uncertain, adverse, or unanticipated conditions. This list includes (but is not limited to) robustness, reliability, resilience, survivability, vulnerability, availability, supportability, maintainability, susceptibility, adaptability, recoverability, stability, and changeability. Depending on the industry and application, the terms may have different or often overlapping meanings, with different relationships to one another. A comprehensive, yet concise overview of these “change-related ‘-ilities’” is given in Ref. 42. In the context of ECLSS design, any effort to improve confidence in life support effectiveness (at maintaining a suitable, habitable spacecraft environment) under increasing levels of uncertainty and risk will require a common semantic frame of reference. This allows designers and stakeholders to use a common contextual language intelligibly,54 in order to adequately express and understand ideas and concepts. Characteristics that may (or may not) contribute to the system effectiveness in an uncertain changing context must be clearly defined, before they can be incorporated into the design process. A review of literature on change-related ‘-ilities’ reveals four emergent terms that best describe system effectiveness in a dynamic uncertain environment, or performance under perturbation: robustness, reliability, resilience, and survivability. With the exception of reliability, the definitions of the other three vary greatly in literature, even within specific industries. Some say that resilience encompasses robustness, and others say the reverse, that resilience is a part of being robust. Many use the terms interchangeably, while others maintain that they are two distinct and even mutually exclusive system characteristics. The same is true for survivability and resilience. In this work, we propose that robustness is actually an over-arching characteristic that captures reliability, resilience, and survivability. 2. Definition of Robustness
The Oxford dictionary defines a robust system as “able to withstand or overcome adverse conditions.” The Merriam Webster dictionary defines robust systems as “capable of performing without failure under a wide range of conditions (in software).” Random House defines it as “strong and effective in all or most situations and conditions.” In technical literature, much like resilience, definitions of robustness for engineering systems vary. Some definitions treat robustness as distinct from resilience, emphasizing an unwavering resistance to disturbance or perturbation, without any change in the system.55,56 The Taguchi method, known in industrial quality engineering, defines a robust design as one that delivers a strong signal regardless of external or internal noise.57 “Robust products can perform intended functions under realistic downstream production and disturbances due to usage, without controlling the sources of disturbance.”57 Many other definitions extend robustness to include the ability to maintain output, function, or features regardless of changes in the system or changes in context.58 Others extend it to allow both passive and active means of maintaining function:
“Robustness is capacity to cope with ordinary and occasional disturbances by avoiding exposure, being resistant, or responding/recovering after disruption.”59
Proposition 1: The concept of robustness is proposed to characterize and improve ECLSS performance. We suggest that ECLSS robustness encompasses all aspects of its mission effectiveness. ECLSS function, or purpose of operation, is to maintain habitability of the spacecraft environment. Therefore, ECLSS robustness is its ability to maintain habitable conditions for crew survival and productivity over the mission lifetime ‘under a wide range of conditions’ (Merriam Webster definition). This wide range of conditions includes ordinary usage, finite occasional disturbances or disruptions, and longer term, sustained changes in the system or mission context. Robustness includes the ability to function during expected circumstances, as well as in a changing environment, or during unanticipated events. It allows for limited degradation of performance as long as crew survival and their ability to perform mission work are not compromised. It includes contingency operations for crew survival during adverse events and emergencies. Proposition 2: Three separate system characteristics that contribute to ECLSS robustness are reliability, resilience, and survivability. ECLSS robustness is the insensitivity of performance (i.e. maintaining habitability) to 1) Random expected failures and conditions (reliability), 2) Foreseen but unexpected deviations in conditions or disturbances (resilience), and 3) Unforeseen disturbances or adverse events (survivability). Each of these characteristics has varying applicability, effectiveness, and cost depending on the mission circumstances. By definition, robustness widens the conditions in which the system performs acceptably beyond


