10
expected (i.e. nominal) to unexpected (less likely) perturbations and disturbances. By its stochastic nature, reliability itself (or likelihood of random failure over the life cycle) is an uncertainty. Therefore, robust encompasses reliable. At a higher level of robustness, the system is resilient to unexpected but foreseeable deviations in conditions. At the highest level of robustness, the system also demonstrates survivability to unforeseeable but plausible conditions and disturbances. Resilience includes the ability to absorb, withstand, or recover from events outside of those it is designed for; and possibly even adapt or change itself to avoid future adverse events. Survivability is the ability to deliver essential services in the face some adverse, typically catastrophic event. Figure 2 depicts reliable, resilient, and survivable as three distinct layers of robustness across a continuum of uncertainty. The further characterization of ECLSS robustness and contributing characteristics will lay the foundation for future work in identifying design features and practices for sustaining operation in the face of the uncertainty of deep space missions.
Figure 2 Robust Characteristics 3. Definition of Reliability
Reliability is herein defined according the SEBoK definition: “The probability of a system or system element performing its intended function under stated conditions without failure for a given period of time.”6 The distinguishing aspect of the SEBoK definition of reliability is performance under “stated conditions” for a given period. Stated conditions are sometimes called nominal. Nominal operating conditions may be static, or they may include some known, well-characterized variability that a component is designed to tolerate, often reflected in specific design requirements. Reliability includes the probability of random failures but also the system availability (with respect to repair and maintenance). In computer science and software engineering, the term dependability (also known as RAMS) is sometimes used. RAMS includes reliability, availability, maintainability, and security. However, in traditional quality engineering, reliability is sufficient to encompass these additional aspects. Hence, for this research the term reliable is chosen, as defined above, and may be treated synonymously with dependable. 4. Definition of Resilience
The Oxford dictionary defines resilience as “the capacity to recover quickly from difficulties; toughness” and “the ability of a substance or object to spring back into shape; elasticity.” The definition of resilience varies widely in the engineering literature. The Department of Homeland Security60 defines resilience as the ability to adapt to changing conditions and prepare for, withstand, and rapidly recover from disruption. Ref. 61 defines resiliency as a superset combining survivability and recoverability. Distinct attributes of resilience include flexibility or elasticity to withstand unexpected failures; the ability and time to recover function after failure or adverse events; and the ability to change or adapt to deal with stresses or changes. Another distinguishing feature is that a service or capability disruption has occurred. Resilience typically involves active responses (flexibility, recovery, and adaptation), as opposed to passive resistance to adversity or perturbation. 5. Definition of Survivability
The concept of survivability has evolved from the domain of military systems and has become an important metric in design of public networks, infrastructure systems, and space systems.61
Survivability may be defined generally as the ability of a system to minimize the impact of a finite disturbance on value delivery, achieved through either the satisfaction of a minimally acceptable level of value delivery during and after a finite disturbance or the reduction of the likelihood or magnitude of a disturbance.62
Distinctive features of this definition are the magnitude of impact of finite disturbances on the system, in terms of either service disruption or impairment. The Department of Defense defines vulnerability and susceptibility as subsets of survivability.63 Resistance and stability are two potentially contributing factors to survivability. In many texts, resistance defines robustness, in the context of output insensitivity to variations of inputs or other environmental


