factors. Resistance is the degree to which a variable changes, following a perturbation.64 Similarly, stability is sometimes synonymous with robustness and resistance to change. Stability is the ability to return to an equilibrium state after a temporary disturbance.65 Stability contributes to resistance, and resistance may contribute to both resilience and survivability.
B. Need for an ECLSS Robustness Metric
Robustness becomes increasingly important as missions venture further from Earth.27,52 Exploration life support systems must continue functioning under harsh conditions, for long durations, without support, and with limited resources or resupply. Due to the increased uncertainty and risk of deep space exploration missions, the ability to maintain function in abnormal, off-nominal conditions as well as nominal conditions is increasingly important. However, ECLSS performance to date falls short of the extreme reliability requirements evidenced by actual failure rates as well as on-board maintenance requirements.32, 47, 50, 66 Ref. 34 suggests that long duration missions must not only have ultra-reliability on the order of 1E-6, but that reliability must also have low variation. Traditional reliability analysis does not account for many factors that affect system performance, including component interactions, tolerances, possible design errors, failure rate uncertainty, operator error, unexpected environmental conditions, partially failed states, buffering capacity, or maintenance or repair strategies.34, 37, 51 Ref. 7 points out that it is important to find a “maximally stable system” that can cope with emergencies without losing stability. The authors describe stability as the ability of the system “not to exceed certain thresholds by itself and the ability to return to a nominal functioning (all values within defined thresholds) after a system disturbance.” The objective for ECLSS performance should expand beyond reliability, or even ultra-reliability, to robustness, in order to reduce the cost and risk of uncertainty for deep space exploration. Many studies have proposed methods for ECLSS reliability estimation and expanded techniques to consider redundancy, maintenance, repair, and buffering. However, the methods reviewed thus far do not account for the range of possible events and disturbances that could affect performance. If robustness is a new optimization objective, then it must be quantifiable for design evaluation and comparison. A quantifiable robustness metric serves three purposes in ECLSS design: design evaluation, design comparison for trades and optimization, and design improvement by allowing identification of robust design features.
“Measurement is the first step that leads to control and eventually to improvement. If you can’t measure something, you can’t understand it. If you can’t understand it, you can’t control it. If you can’t control it, you can’t improve it.” - H. James Harrington67
ESM is a valuable measure of system cost, but should not be the exclusive criteria for system evaluation, especially for missions with increasing demands for robustness. In theory, analysts use ESM to compare systems of equal reliability, which is extensible to equal robustness. However, it is unlikely that ECLSS designs have equal reliability or robustness in practice, due to diligence applied in analysis and the complexity of system designs. If robustness is the more important objective, then it should be in the numerator, rather than the denominator (i.e. performance per cost instead of cost per performance). This brings more focus in design on ways to improve performance, and less focus on mass. Would mission planners want the lowest ESM system that meets a desired, acceptable reliability or the most robust system for an acceptable ESM? Ref. 32 points out that systems procured with a “best performance for a reasonable cost” philosophy, rather than a “lowest cost for a specified performance” approach tend to achieve higher reliability. ESM measures focus system development towards the latter approach, instead of establishing an acceptable cost threshold and designing towards the maximum possible performance. Cost and performance are conflicting optimization objectives. Ideally, analysts should evaluate them separately and present a Pareto optimal set of solutions to decision makers, allowing for shifting cost vs. performance priorities.
C. Suggested Characteristics for a Robustness Metric
Habitability vs. Crew Survival: Current performance metrics focus on failure and crew survival but do not quantify habitability in terms of productive crew time available, which is ultimately the purpose of the life support system. If the intention were only to keep the crew alive, there would be no requirements for crew comfort. A robustness metric might extend the expectation of performance to include productive capacity of the crew, not just the probability of loss of crew (PrLOC). Ref. 7 suggests that astronaut crew time for science [or other mission objectives] is the most important commodity that ECLSS provides, and that an ECLSS optimization goal is to maximize productive crew time available. Disturbances in ECLSS performance accumulate the loss of crew time, including time spent on


