12
maintenance, time lost due to degraded health [including fatality], time to repair the system after a failure, and time lost due to crew error because of malnutrition or physical stress.7 System Levels of Abstraction: Systems have hierarchical levels of abstraction, such as components, assemblies, sub-systems, integrated systems, and systems of systems.54 It is reasonable to expect that a robustness metric be applicable to all levels of system abstraction. In other words, robustness of a valve should have the same definition as robustness of a heat exchanger, or robustness of an air revitalization system. However, improving performance at a lower level may not improve performance at the top level. A collection of the most robust components may not result in the most robust integrated system. Emergent system level properties occur as the result of architectural features, component interfaces, and relationships such as feedback loops. Design Fidelity: A robustness metric should be applicable to varying degrees of design fidelity, allowing analytical rough order approximations during preliminary architecture trades, as well as high fidelity estimates for more detailed designs (through numerical simulations and/or testing). Practicality for Use: A metric must be simple enough that it is practical for use by designers and analysts. It must be understandable, relatively easy to compute, and based on familiar, operationally relevant design features. Relevance and Objectivity: While a metric should not be so complex that it is impractical for use in design, it should include enough detail to provide meaningful, relevant information. It should be precise and objective enough to allow useful technology comparisons and verification through testing. It should account for all factors contributing to robustness without double counting. If possible, the metric should not include subjective weighting factors. Compatibility with Existing Assessment Tools: The metric should have a generic formulation that allows its incorporation into current toolsets. Though the authors propose that a new robustness metric is necessary, it should be supplemental to or build upon existing reliability assessment tools (such as EMAT), ECLSS system modeling and simulation tools, and optimization algorithms. Compatibility with All Technology Types: A robustness metric should be equally applicable to biological, physical, and chemical processes; regenerable and non-regenerable systems; and old and new technologies. A robustness metric must quantify the system’s ability to maintain consistent performance (i.e. conditions necessary for crew productivity) in time, under perturbation of state, and in the event of system disturbance (failure or other shock). Mathematically, robustness might extend the reliability function R(t) from the time domain to include other dimensions of state vector x, R(t,x). A survey of ecological, complex systems, and network theory will unearth potential robustness indicators for consideration. Promising criteria include integral reliability,30 multiplicative probability estimation,35 ecological stability indexes, and the susceptibility index.68 Ref. 38 and Ref. 40 also suggest the use of Markov chains with Monte Carlo simulation to predict ECLSS resilience and objectively model uncertainty for systems with little failure data.
V. Conclusion
Future research will explore methodology for the design of robust environmental control and life support systems (ECLSS) for human exploration of deep space. Research objectives are to define terminology, define metrics, develop assessment methods, and provide design guidance for improving ECLSS robustness. Objective 1. Establish Semantic Frame of Reference: A common contextual language is necessary to adequately express and understand the ideas of systems analysis as it applies to ECLSS design. Establishing common terminology has been the primary goal of this introductory paper. Based on extensive review of literature on the change-related ‘ilities’, the concept of robustness is proposed to characterize and improve ECLSS performance.
ECLSS robustness is herein defined as the ability to maintain habitable conditions for crew survival and productivity over the mission lifetime under a wide range of conditions. We suggest that ECLSS robustness encompasses all aspects of its effective performance (independent of cost), and that three separate system characteristics contribute to ECLSS robustness: reliability, resilience, and survivability.
Objective 2. Define Measurable Criteria for Quantifying ECLSS Robustness: The most commonly used ECLSS performance metrics (ESM, reliability, closure, stability, and integral reliability) do not sufficiently capture robustness. A metric that brings focus on the ECLSS performance rather than cost will bring increased attention to performance improvement. A robustness metric should address spacecraft habitability, not just crew survival; apply to all levels of system abstraction; apply to all design phases or levels of fidelity; be practical for use, relevant, and objective; and be compatible with existing assessment tools and all technology types. The next phase of research in robust ECLSS design will endeavor to develop a robustness metric incorporating these characteristics. In addition to the methods listed herein, a survey of ecological, complex systems, and network theory will unearth potential robustness indicators for consideration.


