Objective 3. Develop and Demonstrate Methods to Assess Robustness: Even with a defined metric, a method of determining its value for a given design will require a consistent and defined assessment process. This will include system definition and modeling, a definition of nominal system performance, definition of the acceptable levels and duration of degraded performance, and characterization of sources and impacts of change or uncertainty that could affect system operation. Methods of bounding, describing, and modeling uncertainty will be necessary for system performance assessment and verification. Existing risk analysis tools such as failure modes and effects analysis (FMEA), fault tree analysis (FTA), and probabilistic risk assessment (PRA) may aid in the identification of likely, possible, and plausible adverse events. Assessment methods will supplement and expand upon existing ECLSS reliability analysis tools such as RELISSA or EMAT.
Objective 4. Provide Guidance for Improving ECLSS Robustness for Exploration Missions: The ultimate goal of this research is to improve ECLSS robustness for long duration distant missions. Metrics and assessment only serve to tell designers how close they are to the achieving the goal but not how to get there. Future work will identify robust design features and recommend best practices for robust ECLSS design. Traditional reliability engineering is not enough to ensure operability under uncertain changing conditions. Efforts to minimize industrial process variation and to ensure the dependability of complex, critical infrastructure (such as communications networks) have resulted in the emergence of survivability analysis, rare events modeling, robust design, and resilience engineering. The application of these specialty engineering disciplines to spacecraft design is somewhat new and their potential for ECLSS design may be yet untapped. A review of systems design literature reveals a variety of characteristics that contribute to system operability during unanticipated conditions, such as redundancy, modularity, failure mode isolation, network connectedness, simplicity, passive control, wide design margins, maintainability, accessibility, interface commonality, capacity for lower levels repair, functional diversity, etc. Lessons learned from the operational history of life support systems to date will also provide invaluable input to improving robustness. Many studies and reports on ECLSS performance cite the need for robust systems. There has been much progress in the definitions, assessment tools, and design practices for ECLSS reliability. However there has been little attention given to the definition, tools, and practices for robust ECLSS design. Sometimes the word robust is used interchangeably with reliable, resilient, and survivable. We propose that robust encompasses the other three distinct and equally important aspects of system performance. This research will not attempt to replace or improve upon the wealth of system modeling and simulation tools developed by ECLSS analysts. Nor will it attempt to create new optimization algorithms or techniques. Instead, the proposed metrics and assessment methods for robust design will supplement existing modeling and optimization methods, expanding their applicability to a wider range of ECLSS design and operation.
